[2026-02-18] PIPELINE OVERVIEW
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Objective : Cross-Platform Profile Matching (Twitter / Instagram / Google+)
  Method    : Sentence-BERT (SBERT) with MPNet backbone
  Model     : all-mpnet-base-v2 (768 dimensions)
  Reference : Social-LLM research paper

================================================================================
  STEP 1: DATA LOADING
================================================================================
  Source file     : data/combined_profiles.csv
  Source size     : ~11.8 MB
  Platforms       : Twitter, Instagram, Google+
  Notes           : Loaded from pre-combined CSV (faster than reading 23,000+ JSON files)

================================================================================
  STEP 2: TEXT CLEANING & PROFILE ENRICHMENT
================================================================================
  Cleaning steps:
    - Fix broken unicode (ftfy)
    - Remove URLs, @mentions
    - Convert #hashtags → plain words
    - Convert emoji → text descriptions (emoji.demojize)
    - Remove zero-width spaces
    - Discard texts shorter than 3 characters

  Profile Enrichment (enrich_profile):
    - bio (most important, placed first)
    - fullName (normalized)
    - location
    - externalUrl (domain only)
    - Combined with " | " separator

  Output columns added:
    - bio_clean     : cleaned bio text
    - enriched_text : combined profile text for embedding

================================================================================
  STEP 3: SBERT-MPNet EMBEDDING GENERATION
================================================================================
  Model           : all-mpnet-base-v2 (Sentence-Transformers)
  Embedding dim   : 768
  Max seq length  : 384 tokens
  Normalization   : L2 normalized (cosine sim = dot product)
  Batch size      : 64
  Data type       : float32

  Individual Embeddings:
    - Total files  : 2,478 per-user embedding files
    - File pattern : {username}_{platform}_embedding.npy
    - File size    : ~2,176 bytes each (768 × float32 = 3,072 bytes + npy header)
    - Location     : data/embeddings/

  Batch Embeddings:
    - File         : data/embeddings/bio_mpnet_embeddings.npy
    - Size         : ~71.9 MB
    - Shape        : (N_profiles, 768)

================================================================================
  STEP 4: FAISS INDEX
================================================================================
  Index type      : IndexFlatIP (Inner Product → cosine similarity)
  Dimension       : 768
  Output file     : data/embeddings/bio_mpnet_faiss.index
  File size       : ~71.9 MB

================================================================================
  STEP 5: CROSS-PLATFORM MATCHING
================================================================================
  Matching Strategy:
    - Stage 1: FAISS retrieval of top-k candidates (top_k=20)
    - Stage 2: Weighted multi-feature scoring

  Scoring Weights (following Social-LLM research):
    - bio_semantic  (SBERT cosine sim) : 50%
    - name_sim      (RapidFuzz token sort) : 25%
    - username_sim  (RapidFuzz partial) : 15%
    - location+url  (max of location & URL match) : 10%

  Platform Pairs Matched:
    1. Twitter  → Instagram
    2. Twitter  → Google+
    3. Instagram → Google+

  Threshold       : 0.45 (minimum final_score for a match)
  Confidence levels:
    - Very High : >= 0.70
    - High      : >= 0.55
    - Medium    : >= 0.45
    - Low       : < 0.45 (filtered out)

================================================================================
  STEP 6: OUTPUT FILES
================================================================================

  Embeddings:
    data/embeddings/bio_mpnet_embeddings.npy      ~71.9 MB   (batch embedding vectors)
    data/embeddings/bio_mpnet_faiss.index          ~71.9 MB   (FAISS search index)
    data/embeddings/bio_mpnet_metadata.csv          ~7.5 MB   (index-to-profile mapping)

  Matching Results:
    data/matching/cross_platform_matches.csv        ~5.5 MB   (all matches >= 0.45)
    data/matching/high_confidence_matches.csv        ~3.2 MB   (matches >= 0.55 only)

  Enriched Data:
    data/profiles_with_enriched_text.csv           ~18.3 MB   (profiles + enriched text)

  Visualization:
    data/matching_results_chart.png                ~104 KB    (score distribution charts)

================================================================================
  STEP 7: KNOWN ISSUES & BUG FIXES
================================================================================

  [BUG] IndexError in find_same_person() — 2026-02-18
  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Symptom : IndexError: invalid index to scalar variable
            when calling find_same_person(first_with_bio, top_k=10)

  Cause   : df_valid.index[0] returns np.int64, which does NOT pass
            isinstance(value, int) check in some numpy versions.
            The function falls into the 'else' branch that treats
            the integer as a string → model.encode() fails.

  Fix     : Change isinstance check to include numpy integer types:
            BEFORE: isinstance(profile_idx_or_bio, int)
            AFTER : isinstance(profile_idx_or_bio, (int, np.integer))

            Also cast: idx = int(profile_idx_or_bio) for safety.

  Status  : Identified, fix documented (apply in notebook)

================================================================================
  USAGE: How to reload saved results (no re-encoding needed)
================================================================================

  import numpy as np
  import pandas as pd
  import faiss
  from sentence_transformers import SentenceTransformer

  # Load model
  model = SentenceTransformer('all-mpnet-base-v2')

  # Load embeddings + index
  embeddings = np.load('data/embeddings/bio_mpnet_embeddings.npy')
  index = faiss.read_index('data/embeddings/bio_mpnet_faiss.index')
  metadata = pd.read_csv('data/embeddings/bio_mpnet_metadata.csv')

  # Search
  query = model.encode(['AI researcher Bangkok'], normalize_embeddings=True)
  scores, indices = index.search(query.astype('float32'), 10)

  # Load matching results
  matches = pd.read_csv('data/matching/cross_platform_matches.csv')

